WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:11.410
BRIAN SCHWARZ:
Welcome to the What's

00:00:11.410 --> 00:00:13.900
New and What's Next for
Storage session here

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:16.239
at Google Cloud Next '21.

00:00:16.239 --> 00:00:17.620
My name is Brian Schwarz.

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:19.925
And I'm joined by my colleague
here, Dave Nettleton.

00:00:19.925 --> 00:00:21.550
We're leaders in the
product management

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:23.050
team here for
storage, and excited

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:25.450
to talk to you about the
entire storage portfolio

00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:28.870
that Dave and I work on
along with our colleagues.

00:00:28.870 --> 00:00:31.090
During the session
today we've broken it up

00:00:31.090 --> 00:00:32.870
into a few different sections.

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:35.530
First, Dave's going to start
with the strategy for Google

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:36.113
Cloud Storage.

00:00:36.113 --> 00:00:38.530
And then we're going to run
through the updates and what's

00:00:38.530 --> 00:00:41.470
new on a number of parts of the
product line, Cloud Storage,

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:43.120
file, persistent disk.

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:45.970
And we'll end with some
discussions about transfer

00:00:45.970 --> 00:00:49.840
in Backup Archive DR, as well
as include a few links to where

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:51.495
to find more information.

00:00:51.495 --> 00:00:52.870
With that, I want
to turn it over

00:00:52.870 --> 00:00:57.280
to Dave to start a discussion
about the strategy.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:58.530
DAVE NETTLETON: Thanks, Brian.

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:00.098
And so just at a
very high level,

00:01:00.098 --> 00:01:01.890
I thought it'd be
helpful to go through how

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:04.660
we think about our strategy and
the different parts of that.

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:07.140
So our goal is to
deliver easy to use,

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:09.930
performant, and highly available
storage for all workloads

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:13.230
with enterprise management
controls and insights.

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:16.468
This really guides how we
think about both the portfolio

00:01:16.468 --> 00:01:18.510
of products that we have,
but also the individual

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:20.370
features within them.

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:23.370
And so at a high level,
three main types of storage

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:27.450
that we provide in the Cloud,
block, object, and file.

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:30.105
We'll spend a little bit
more time on each of these.

00:01:30.105 --> 00:01:34.410
These cover both cloud native
and more traditional enterprise

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:35.310
use cases.

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:38.400
Together with those,
we have capabilities

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:41.340
for data transfer and
data protection, which

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:44.070
are very important
services that come hand

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:46.840
in hand often with storage.

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:49.710
And then as well as what we
deliver in terms of first party

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:51.990
products from Google,
we're very big

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:55.320
believers in having an open
ecosystem of partners here

00:01:55.320 --> 00:02:01.560
to give customers a range of
choices for their workloads

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:03.150
as they bring them to the cloud.

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:06.300
Two big categories there would
be in file and data protection.

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:11.039
For file we partner closely
with NetApp, Dell EMC, and DDN.

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:13.230
And for Backup and DR,
Commvault, and Veeam

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:15.750
are two of the partners,
who amongst others

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:18.397
we work with very, very closely.

00:02:18.397 --> 00:02:19.980
So let me dive in a
little bit on some

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:22.170
of the great
enhancements that we've

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:24.900
had in the products recently.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:29.730
So starting with our object
storage product, Cloud Storage.

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:32.790
So one of the great
features in Cloud Storage,

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:35.070
our object storage
product, is the notion

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:37.740
of dual regional storage.

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:41.910
And what this does is
it lets you manage data

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:42.960
across two regions.

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:45.330
So without this
product, you need

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:48.630
to put a bucket in
each of two regions.

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:51.540
Each of those buckets would
have a different name.

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:54.750
You'd have to separately deal
with managing and copying data

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:57.090
from one bucket to the other.

00:02:57.090 --> 00:02:58.800
If you had to deal
with a disaster

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:00.300
or you wanted to
redirect traffic

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:02.320
to read from one
bucket or the other,

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:04.620
you'd have to deal with
failover and fail back

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:07.050
of those storage buckets.

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:13.680
With dual regional storage,
we provide a product

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:16.950
that gives just a single bucket
that spans those two regions.

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:19.530
So resources in each
of those regions,

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:22.500
or indeed, in other regions,
can read data from that bucket.

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:26.100
And we will manage, behind the
scenes, the data placement.

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:28.228
And we provide a
single namespace

00:03:28.228 --> 00:03:29.520
that you can read and write to.

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:35.050
So it's active, active from
a read and write perspective.

00:03:35.050 --> 00:03:37.920
We provide strong consistency
over that metadata

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:38.640
of the bucket.

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:41.610
And then in the background,
we move the data,

00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:43.920
replicate the data
between the two regions.

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:47.190
In the event of a failure
in one of the regions,

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:48.960
the application won't
notice it continue

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:52.590
to read the metadata that's
consistent across these two

00:03:52.590 --> 00:04:01.080
regions, giving immediate
failover to any activity that's

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:04.050
happening in the other region.

00:04:04.050 --> 00:04:09.360
One of the big enhancements
that we are announcing

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:12.433
is that we're going to allow
not just fixed name pairs, which

00:04:12.433 --> 00:04:13.350
is what we have today.

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:15.520
We currently have a
pair of regions in Asia,

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:19.920
a pair of regions in the US,
and a pair of regions in Europe.

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:21.990
We're going to allow
customers to specify

00:04:21.990 --> 00:04:25.500
the particular regions that
they want to create a custom

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:26.340
dual region across.

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:28.650
This is very powerful,
being able to pair together

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:30.630
a couple of regions and
provide a single bucket

00:04:30.630 --> 00:04:32.370
that spans those two regions.

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:36.180
Together with that, we also
want to deliver a capability

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:39.330
to give stronger guarantees
around the recovery point

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:39.880
objective.

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:42.150
So we call this turbo
replication, where

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:47.610
we'll give an SLA of the data
that is written on one side

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:51.425
will be replicated to the
other side within 15 minutes.

00:04:51.425 --> 00:04:55.980
And so we'll provide
an SLA back replication

00:04:55.980 --> 00:04:58.740
for the data in these
dual region buckets.

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:01.740
Very excited about this in
helping customers better

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:03.870
support their business
continuity needs,

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:07.380
and also managing their data
across multiple regions.

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:10.530
As well as that feature,
some other enhancements

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:14.290
from Cloud Storage that
have happened recently.

00:05:14.290 --> 00:05:17.003
So multipart uploads, this
has been a very common feature

00:05:17.003 --> 00:05:18.420
ask from customers
who are looking

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:25.170
to make it easy to bring
large objects to the cloud,

00:05:25.170 --> 00:05:28.410
and construct them in parts,
and finalize them in the clouds.

00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:31.890
This has been a very popular
programmability feature.

00:05:31.890 --> 00:05:34.970
And then a category, the next
three all around security.

00:05:34.970 --> 00:05:38.260
So security is something we
take very, very seriously.

00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:41.500
We work a lot with customers
around their security needs.

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:44.370
So we want to make sure that we
support the various workloads

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:47.460
that they have here,
through assured workloads,

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:52.290
through providing tags, bucket
level tags that let customers

00:05:52.290 --> 00:05:58.440
reason about their data
through IAM policies,

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:03.060
and support
certifications like FINRA

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:04.560
with capabilities
like bucket lock

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:07.530
that let customers lock a
bucket for an extended duration

00:06:07.530 --> 00:06:08.340
of time.

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:11.190
And then another enhancement
that we announced earlier in

00:06:11.190 --> 00:06:11.758
this year--

00:06:11.758 --> 00:06:13.800
it's relatively small,
but actually very powerful

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:17.550
is with Google Cloud Storage we
offer four classes of storage,

00:06:17.550 --> 00:06:20.550
from hot all the
way down to archive.

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:23.070
And we let customers
tier between that data

00:06:23.070 --> 00:06:23.970
for managing costs.

00:06:23.970 --> 00:06:26.160
It doesn't actually--
all storage classes

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:29.080
have the same API and the
same fast access to data.

00:06:29.080 --> 00:06:32.500
So even the archive tier is
online and instantly available.

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:34.800
But customers want a tier
between those storage classes

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:36.690
for cost purposes.

00:06:36.690 --> 00:06:40.530
With a custom timestamp
as part of the metadata,

00:06:40.530 --> 00:06:43.590
customers can now
use a measure of time

00:06:43.590 --> 00:06:46.260
to help them make decisions
about when to tier data.

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:48.900
So it's a powerful capability.

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:50.910
Moving on from
our object storage

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:53.400
and cloud storage
over to file storage,

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:56.280
both from Filestore,
our first party product,

00:06:56.280 --> 00:06:58.360
and also with partners.

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:02.432
So file storage
is a huge use case

00:07:02.432 --> 00:07:04.140
for our customers,
particularly customers

00:07:04.140 --> 00:07:08.100
coming from on premises,
where they have built

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:12.390
rich and sophisticated
workflows around enterprise file

00:07:12.390 --> 00:07:15.720
offerings, whether that's
just as traditional file

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:18.990
shares, or as directories
where they're putting shared

00:07:18.990 --> 00:07:21.930
executables as
part of workflows,

00:07:21.930 --> 00:07:24.820
build pipelines, et cetera.

00:07:24.820 --> 00:07:26.820
So customers are looking,
as they lift and shift

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:30.300
to the cloud, to have a
very powerful set of file

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:31.800
capabilities.

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:35.220
To date, we've had a
file store product that's

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:37.440
both a basic and a high scale.

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:39.090
And these have been
zonal products.

00:07:39.090 --> 00:07:41.430
And we're really
pleased to announce

00:07:41.430 --> 00:07:45.180
that we've launched
Filestore Enterprise, which

00:07:45.180 --> 00:07:46.293
is a regional service.

00:07:46.293 --> 00:07:47.710
So it will survive
a zone failure.

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:49.887
So we replicate data
across multiple zones.

00:07:49.887 --> 00:07:51.720
So customers don't need
to worry about that.

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:53.820
They have a highly
available regional service,

00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:56.070
backed with a four nines SLA.

00:07:56.070 --> 00:07:58.140
And then we're also making
sure that this is then

00:07:58.140 --> 00:08:00.570
well integrated with all of
the other enterprise features

00:08:00.570 --> 00:08:02.028
that customers
expect in the cloud,

00:08:02.028 --> 00:08:04.560
whether that's
Stackdriver for logging,

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:10.050
or our security features like
VPC Service Controls, VPN,

00:08:10.050 --> 00:08:13.447
Interconnect to help customers
set up the appropriate security

00:08:13.447 --> 00:08:15.030
perimeter around
their infrastructure,

00:08:15.030 --> 00:08:17.798
and have that file product
to be part of that.

00:08:17.798 --> 00:08:19.215
And we also want
to make sure this

00:08:19.215 --> 00:08:22.470
is available through our
console, CLI, nice and easy

00:08:22.470 --> 00:08:23.290
to use.

00:08:23.290 --> 00:08:25.710
And then in particular
with GKE, to help

00:08:25.710 --> 00:08:32.309
make it easy to provision
and get file resources there,

00:08:32.309 --> 00:08:36.250
providing the CSI driver
support to simplify.

00:08:36.250 --> 00:08:37.890
So as well as
Filestore Enterprise,

00:08:37.890 --> 00:08:43.110
some other enhancements for file
that we've announced recently

00:08:43.110 --> 00:08:45.720
is backups for Filestore Basic.

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:47.760
So Filestore Basic
is our zonal product.

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:49.860
And we've delivered
backups for that.

00:08:52.530 --> 00:08:57.030
Filestore High Scale is
our high performance scale

00:08:57.030 --> 00:09:01.920
of Filestore, which lets
customers scale to many tens

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:06.025
to up to 100 terabytes of scale
at much higher performance.

00:09:06.025 --> 00:09:08.400
And then as well as what we've
been doing our first party

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:10.590
with Filestore,
lots of enhancements

00:09:10.590 --> 00:09:13.470
through our partners, in
particular with NetApp

00:09:13.470 --> 00:09:14.250
and Dell.

00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:16.230
And we're seeing a lot
of customers having

00:09:16.230 --> 00:09:19.083
a lot of success with
some of the workloads

00:09:19.083 --> 00:09:21.000
that they're bringing
to the cloud with things

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:26.550
like SMB workloads for
Windows, or media rendering

00:09:26.550 --> 00:09:28.388
and genomics.

00:09:28.388 --> 00:09:30.180
And with that, I'll
hand back over to Brian

00:09:30.180 --> 00:09:34.963
to tell you all a little
bit about persistent disk.

00:09:34.963 --> 00:09:36.130
BRIAN SCHWARZ: Thanks, Dave.

00:09:36.130 --> 00:09:39.400
So persistent disk is the
block storage offering here

00:09:39.400 --> 00:09:40.390
in Google Cloud.

00:09:40.390 --> 00:09:43.662
It's often used with
the Compute Engine VMs.

00:09:43.662 --> 00:09:45.370
That's probably the
most common use case,

00:09:45.370 --> 00:09:48.640
but it's also used quite a bit
with Kubernetes Engine, GKE

00:09:48.640 --> 00:09:50.290
for container workloads.

00:09:50.290 --> 00:09:53.540
And it's also used with Cloud
SQL for the managed database

00:09:53.540 --> 00:09:54.040
offering.

00:09:54.040 --> 00:09:57.140
So pretty popular offering, a
bunch of different use cases.

00:09:57.140 --> 00:10:00.490
One of the new, great features
that we introduced recently

00:10:00.490 --> 00:10:04.390
is a new class of performance,
extreme persistent disk.

00:10:04.390 --> 00:10:08.200
And it's really targeted at the
highest performing workloads

00:10:08.200 --> 00:10:10.720
in your data center,
particularly high end

00:10:10.720 --> 00:10:12.970
databases, SAP HANA
being an absolutely

00:10:12.970 --> 00:10:15.340
classic example of this.

00:10:15.340 --> 00:10:17.530
In addition to
great performance,

00:10:17.530 --> 00:10:19.810
another great feature
it has is the ability

00:10:19.810 --> 00:10:22.700
to independently scale
capacity and performance.

00:10:22.700 --> 00:10:27.190
So you can really tune the cost,
and performance, and capacity

00:10:27.190 --> 00:10:29.890
to really get the best TCO
for your environment, just

00:10:29.890 --> 00:10:32.680
in the persistent
disk volumes itself.

00:10:32.680 --> 00:10:35.350
It also includes easy
to use snapshots,

00:10:35.350 --> 00:10:37.430
which can be used for
like cloning use cases.

00:10:37.430 --> 00:10:41.320
So you could refresh test and
dev environments and workflows

00:10:41.320 --> 00:10:43.210
similar to that.

00:10:43.210 --> 00:10:46.120
And also, you can attach
persistent disk volumes

00:10:46.120 --> 00:10:47.410
to any size VM.

00:10:47.410 --> 00:10:51.160
So again, it's another
capability of flexibility.

00:10:51.160 --> 00:10:53.830
So you can really use the
Google Cloud infrastructure

00:10:53.830 --> 00:10:57.430
to tune the capabilities
of the infrastructure

00:10:57.430 --> 00:10:58.870
to exactly what you need.

00:10:58.870 --> 00:11:01.990
So you don't pay for maybe
a really large VM just

00:11:01.990 --> 00:11:04.820
because you need really
high performance storage.

00:11:04.820 --> 00:11:07.930
So again, just try and give
you all that flexibility.

00:11:07.930 --> 00:11:09.250
Dave mentioned security.

00:11:09.250 --> 00:11:12.680
It kind of exists kind of
throughout our portfolio.

00:11:12.680 --> 00:11:15.400
Another classic example is
here with persistent disk

00:11:15.400 --> 00:11:17.590
is the ability to
have, of course,

00:11:17.590 --> 00:11:20.230
at rest encryption and
encryption in transit,

00:11:20.230 --> 00:11:22.450
and lots of flexibility
in terms of how

00:11:22.450 --> 00:11:25.540
you manage the
keys, with customer

00:11:25.540 --> 00:11:27.940
managed keys or
customer supplied keys.

00:11:27.940 --> 00:11:30.100
And another thing we're
really excited about

00:11:30.100 --> 00:11:33.730
is these constant investments
we make in the resiliency

00:11:33.730 --> 00:11:34.510
of our system.

00:11:34.510 --> 00:11:38.420
And extreme persistent disk
has a six nines durability,

00:11:38.420 --> 00:11:42.220
which is a measure of how
durable the storage is.

00:11:42.220 --> 00:11:44.110
It's really important
that we invest

00:11:44.110 --> 00:11:47.147
in making these things
available and durable for you.

00:11:47.147 --> 00:11:48.730
There's a number of
other enhancements

00:11:48.730 --> 00:11:51.880
that we've had for
persistent disk that

00:11:51.880 --> 00:11:54.310
expand beyond extreme PD.

00:11:54.310 --> 00:11:56.650
And the first one
is probably a tie

00:11:56.650 --> 00:11:58.840
off to what I just
talked about durability.

00:11:58.840 --> 00:12:01.150
We have a nice public
blog posting we've done

00:12:01.150 --> 00:12:03.580
and inclusion in some
more documentation

00:12:03.580 --> 00:12:06.940
for PD about the really
leading durability

00:12:06.940 --> 00:12:10.820
guarantees that we have
included in our services.

00:12:10.820 --> 00:12:12.940
Another service that we
added earlier in the year

00:12:12.940 --> 00:12:14.200
was balance PD.

00:12:14.200 --> 00:12:16.043
It's an SSD class storage.

00:12:16.043 --> 00:12:17.710
So it gives you good
performance, not as

00:12:17.710 --> 00:12:20.950
good as extreme PD, but at
a much lower price point.

00:12:20.950 --> 00:12:23.500
And it's a really good
offering for a lot of,

00:12:23.500 --> 00:12:25.510
I'll say general purpose
workloads in the cloud.

00:12:25.510 --> 00:12:29.050
People use them for boot disks,
for test and dev databases.

00:12:29.050 --> 00:12:31.600
There's lots of reasons why
these things are interesting.

00:12:31.600 --> 00:12:37.150
And it really gives you great
IOPS at a lower price point.

00:12:37.150 --> 00:12:39.490
Also with balanced PD
is we have something

00:12:39.490 --> 00:12:42.250
we refer to as regional
PD, which essentially

00:12:42.250 --> 00:12:45.940
is synchronous replication
across the zones in a given

00:12:45.940 --> 00:12:47.080
region.

00:12:47.080 --> 00:12:48.790
This is a differentiated
capability

00:12:48.790 --> 00:12:52.720
for Google Cloud, super powerful
and important for those of you

00:12:52.720 --> 00:12:56.320
who basically need to
protect against zone failures

00:12:56.320 --> 00:12:58.840
and essentially you're
getting synchronous rights

00:12:58.840 --> 00:13:01.000
across multiple regions.

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:03.700
Used quite a bit with the
Cloud SQL offering today

00:13:03.700 --> 00:13:07.030
to provide the highest
levels of availability

00:13:07.030 --> 00:13:09.970
for some of your
important databases.

00:13:09.970 --> 00:13:12.370
And, of course,
along the same lines,

00:13:12.370 --> 00:13:15.790
increasingly investing
in APIs to be

00:13:15.790 --> 00:13:19.390
able to deliver application
consistent snapshots,

00:13:19.390 --> 00:13:21.400
which will really
speed the recovery time

00:13:21.400 --> 00:13:25.420
if you need to re-instantiate
a database from a snapshot.

00:13:25.420 --> 00:13:27.940
Another small part of the
block storage product line

00:13:27.940 --> 00:13:30.280
here is our local SSD offering.

00:13:30.280 --> 00:13:32.350
And you can think about
this as a counterpoint

00:13:32.350 --> 00:13:36.310
to persistent disk that's really
targeted at ephemeral or cache

00:13:36.310 --> 00:13:37.150
workloads.

00:13:37.150 --> 00:13:42.100
So locally attached to the VM,
very high performance, very

00:13:42.100 --> 00:13:44.050
high IOPS, very low latency.

00:13:44.050 --> 00:13:46.510
Really targeted at
these caching workloads,

00:13:46.510 --> 00:13:49.930
in-memory databases where
you need the absolute highest

00:13:49.930 --> 00:13:53.170
level of performance, but you
don't need the persistence,

00:13:53.170 --> 00:13:55.540
and durability,
and the enterprise

00:13:55.540 --> 00:13:57.830
features that we have
in persistent disk.

00:13:57.830 --> 00:14:00.135
So again, a nice addition
to the product line

00:14:00.135 --> 00:14:01.510
earlier this year
was the support

00:14:01.510 --> 00:14:03.880
for 9 terabyte local SSDs.

00:14:03.880 --> 00:14:06.370
And again, like I mentioned
earlier, the ability

00:14:06.370 --> 00:14:09.460
to attach these to many
sizes and shapes of VMs

00:14:09.460 --> 00:14:13.600
so you can really, again, tune
performance with CPU, memory,

00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:16.570
and storage performance, all
being targeted exactly what you

00:14:16.570 --> 00:14:18.497
need for your workload.

00:14:18.497 --> 00:14:20.830
And with that, I would like
to turn it back over to Dave

00:14:20.830 --> 00:14:23.060
to talk about data transfer.

00:14:23.060 --> 00:14:24.310
DAVE NETTLETON: Thanks, Brian.

00:14:24.310 --> 00:14:27.610
Yeah, so data transfer
there's two major products

00:14:27.610 --> 00:14:31.330
in the portfolio that we have
here, the Transfer Appliance

00:14:31.330 --> 00:14:32.630
and the Transfer Service.

00:14:32.630 --> 00:14:34.630
So starting out with
Transfer Appliance,

00:14:34.630 --> 00:14:37.310
one exciting enhancement
that we've had

00:14:37.310 --> 00:14:40.700
is to allow this to be available
to customers in what we

00:14:40.700 --> 00:14:42.200
call a connected mode.

00:14:42.200 --> 00:14:43.970
So to date, we've
had an appliance

00:14:43.970 --> 00:14:48.770
that ships to a
customer's premises,

00:14:48.770 --> 00:14:50.888
either, for example, to
move large amounts of data

00:14:50.888 --> 00:14:53.180
from a data center that
they're migrating to the cloud.

00:14:53.180 --> 00:14:56.510
Or increasingly we're seeing for
capture at the edge use cases,

00:14:56.510 --> 00:14:59.493
where for AI/ML workloads
there's a lot of data capture

00:14:59.493 --> 00:15:00.785
that might happen in the field.

00:15:00.785 --> 00:15:02.600
And we want to gather
a lot of that data

00:15:02.600 --> 00:15:04.970
and then bring it to the cloud.

00:15:04.970 --> 00:15:09.140
So we've had a
Transfer Appliance

00:15:09.140 --> 00:15:10.790
to allow customers
to capture that data

00:15:10.790 --> 00:15:12.140
and ship it to the cloud.

00:15:12.140 --> 00:15:15.260
Recently we just announced
that we'll actually allow this

00:15:15.260 --> 00:15:16.580
to operate in a connected mode.

00:15:16.580 --> 00:15:19.670
So you can actually drop
data onto the appliance,

00:15:19.670 --> 00:15:21.560
and then over a
network connection

00:15:21.560 --> 00:15:24.920
it would move the data to
the cloud on your behalf

00:15:24.920 --> 00:15:27.060
via that connection.

00:15:27.060 --> 00:15:28.800
So excited to see
some of the use cases

00:15:28.800 --> 00:15:31.280
our customers will put this to.

00:15:31.280 --> 00:15:34.250
As well as the Appliance, a lot
of enhancements on the Transfer

00:15:34.250 --> 00:15:36.020
Service recently.

00:15:36.020 --> 00:15:39.770
Several here that
I'll highlight.

00:15:39.770 --> 00:15:42.560
First of all, the
Transfer Service API.

00:15:42.560 --> 00:15:47.543
So giving programmability
control over transfers we think

00:15:47.543 --> 00:15:49.460
will help customers much
better integrate this

00:15:49.460 --> 00:15:52.430
into their various workflows.

00:15:52.430 --> 00:15:54.170
Agent pools is a
capability we've

00:15:54.170 --> 00:15:56.780
added to the agent that
is part of transfer

00:15:56.780 --> 00:15:58.170
service for on premises.

00:15:58.170 --> 00:15:59.930
So this is something
that is deployed

00:15:59.930 --> 00:16:04.220
into a compute resource
and manages the transfer.

00:16:04.220 --> 00:16:10.040
With agent rules, we're now
enabling those resources

00:16:10.040 --> 00:16:13.280
to be better managed across
multiple transfer jobs,

00:16:13.280 --> 00:16:16.550
and over a shared resource
like a network link,

00:16:16.550 --> 00:16:19.940
so giving much more flexibility
control over transfer.

00:16:19.940 --> 00:16:23.360
And then more sources and
sinks for their service.

00:16:23.360 --> 00:16:25.400
We've added support
for Azure Data Lake V2.

00:16:25.400 --> 00:16:28.250
And for the appliance we're
adding more locations,

00:16:28.250 --> 00:16:32.630
with London being one that's
recently been announced.

00:16:32.630 --> 00:16:35.793
And with that, I'll
hand back to Brian.

00:16:35.793 --> 00:16:36.960
BRIAN SCHWARZ: Thanks, Dave.

00:16:36.960 --> 00:16:38.460
For the last content
section, I want

00:16:38.460 --> 00:16:40.365
to talk about Backup,
Archive, and DR.

00:16:40.365 --> 00:16:42.240
And it's a really
important area to make sure

00:16:42.240 --> 00:16:45.600
that you're protecting your data
in all the different places it

00:16:45.600 --> 00:16:46.920
exists in Google Cloud.

00:16:46.920 --> 00:16:48.900
One of the new
offerings that we just

00:16:48.900 --> 00:16:52.530
announced in September
of '21 is a new offering

00:16:52.530 --> 00:16:55.890
focused on containers,
Google Cloud GKE Backup.

00:16:55.890 --> 00:16:57.765
And what we've
seen is an increase

00:16:57.765 --> 00:17:01.080
in the number of customers
running stateful workloads

00:17:01.080 --> 00:17:05.292
inside of containers, including
very traditional databases.

00:17:05.292 --> 00:17:06.750
And as a result of
that, we've been

00:17:06.750 --> 00:17:09.359
working on essentially a
new service offering that's

00:17:09.359 --> 00:17:11.819
targeted at protecting the
data inside these stateful

00:17:11.819 --> 00:17:13.200
containers.

00:17:13.200 --> 00:17:15.390
It has a nice
orchestration built into it

00:17:15.390 --> 00:17:18.780
that essentially allows you
to take regular PD snapshots

00:17:18.780 --> 00:17:21.960
to protect the data inside
these stateful containers.

00:17:21.960 --> 00:17:24.329
And it gives you pretty
flexible recovery options

00:17:24.329 --> 00:17:27.750
to recover individual
containers,

00:17:27.750 --> 00:17:31.470
as well as full clusters, both
in the normal region where

00:17:31.470 --> 00:17:34.470
it was running, as well as
potentially for a DR use case

00:17:34.470 --> 00:17:37.560
to reinstantiate it
inside of another region.

00:17:37.560 --> 00:17:40.020
There is flexibility
to basically manage

00:17:40.020 --> 00:17:43.770
your backup policy, scheduling,
recurring snapshots,

00:17:43.770 --> 00:17:46.350
as well as setting the
retention cycles on how long

00:17:46.350 --> 00:17:49.320
you want to keep these
snapshots around.

00:17:49.320 --> 00:17:52.170
And as GKE and
containers continues

00:17:52.170 --> 00:17:54.990
to be used for more
important, very traditional,

00:17:54.990 --> 00:17:58.410
enterprise class workloads,
it's important to have offerings

00:17:58.410 --> 00:17:59.970
like this that
essentially help you

00:17:59.970 --> 00:18:01.750
meet compliance requirements.

00:18:01.750 --> 00:18:04.710
So you can create
backups for the data

00:18:04.710 --> 00:18:06.810
for inside of these containers.

00:18:06.810 --> 00:18:08.820
And we also have the
ability to do pre and post

00:18:08.820 --> 00:18:10.500
hooks so you can
also get application

00:18:10.500 --> 00:18:13.850
consistent snapshots.

00:18:13.850 --> 00:18:16.575
Across the rest of our
Backup and Archive portfolio,

00:18:16.575 --> 00:18:18.950
there's a number of other
things that have been going on.

00:18:18.950 --> 00:18:21.860
In particular, last
year at the end of 2020

00:18:21.860 --> 00:18:23.270
we acquired Actifio.

00:18:23.270 --> 00:18:26.450
And it's the real centerpiece
of our first party backup

00:18:26.450 --> 00:18:28.580
investments going forward.

00:18:28.580 --> 00:18:30.800
Actifio was a
Google Cloud partner

00:18:30.800 --> 00:18:33.800
and had an offering called
Actifio Go in the marketplace.

00:18:33.800 --> 00:18:36.290
And we've been working
on making that offering

00:18:36.290 --> 00:18:38.210
more native inside
of Google Cloud,

00:18:38.210 --> 00:18:41.660
to really simplify the
deployment of this service,

00:18:41.660 --> 00:18:42.590
integrated billing.

00:18:42.590 --> 00:18:45.470
And, of course, it leverages
all the great technology

00:18:45.470 --> 00:18:47.540
Actifio had as it
came into Google

00:18:47.540 --> 00:18:50.060
around incremental
forevers in terms

00:18:50.060 --> 00:18:52.790
of speeding up the backups
after the first one.

00:18:52.790 --> 00:18:55.010
It's really targeted,
again, at these mission

00:18:55.010 --> 00:19:00.000
critical databases, SAP HANA,
Oracle, MySQL, et cetera.

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:05.540
And it's really important for us
to continue to invest in this,

00:19:05.540 --> 00:19:08.660
particularly about making it
a first class service that'll

00:19:08.660 --> 00:19:10.700
be more natively
integrated into some

00:19:10.700 --> 00:19:14.000
of the higher level managed
services as we go on.

00:19:14.000 --> 00:19:16.177
And to round out this, I
wanted to briefly mention

00:19:16.177 --> 00:19:18.260
some of the partners, just
like Dave did for file.

00:19:18.260 --> 00:19:20.093
There's a number of
data protection partners

00:19:20.093 --> 00:19:22.010
we work with, two of
them who have done

00:19:22.010 --> 00:19:24.650
some recent enhancements
to the offerings

00:19:24.650 --> 00:19:26.870
to support Google Cloud
are Veeam and Commvault.

00:19:26.870 --> 00:19:30.870
So Veeam V11, which
is often used on prem,

00:19:30.870 --> 00:19:33.890
now supports Google Cloud
Storage as a backup target.

00:19:33.890 --> 00:19:37.220
So you can really think about
the tape replacement use case.

00:19:37.220 --> 00:19:40.040
I like to tell people, one
of the hidden gems of GCS

00:19:40.040 --> 00:19:42.170
is some of the cheaper
tiers of storage

00:19:42.170 --> 00:19:44.340
have very low latency
in terms of access.

00:19:44.340 --> 00:19:47.990
So you can really use these
cheaper tiers to protect

00:19:47.990 --> 00:19:50.690
your data and have compliance.

00:19:50.690 --> 00:19:52.190
But if you ever
need to get it back,

00:19:52.190 --> 00:19:56.210
you don't need to wait hours
or days to do restores.

00:19:56.210 --> 00:20:00.380
And also, there is a new Veeam
for Backup for Google, V2

00:20:00.380 --> 00:20:02.300
that just got released
on the marketplace.

00:20:02.300 --> 00:20:06.680
And it also supports the
archive class tiering,

00:20:06.680 --> 00:20:10.280
which, again, will reduce the
cost for long term retention.

00:20:10.280 --> 00:20:12.590
And lastly, Commvault
has done a great job.

00:20:12.590 --> 00:20:14.090
They're a member
of our marketplace,

00:20:14.090 --> 00:20:15.920
recently came onto
the marketplace.

00:20:15.920 --> 00:20:17.840
And has some great capabilities.

00:20:17.840 --> 00:20:20.570
Obviously well known
for VMware backups,

00:20:20.570 --> 00:20:23.510
and have done some nice
integration with Google Cloud

00:20:23.510 --> 00:20:24.320
VMware Engine.

00:20:24.320 --> 00:20:27.260
So again, an exciting
new partnership.

00:20:27.260 --> 00:20:29.930
We're excited to work with these
partners as well as others,

00:20:29.930 --> 00:20:33.020
again, to provide a wide array
of backup, archive, and DR

00:20:33.020 --> 00:20:36.650
solutions, both from Google
and from our partner ecosystem.

00:20:36.650 --> 00:20:38.690
So to round out
the session, I just

00:20:38.690 --> 00:20:40.700
wanted to talk a
little bit about where

00:20:40.700 --> 00:20:42.537
to learn more information.

00:20:42.537 --> 00:20:44.120
And there's a couple
of other sessions

00:20:44.120 --> 00:20:46.400
here at Google Cloud
Next that I think

00:20:46.400 --> 00:20:48.320
if you're interested
in this section would

00:20:48.320 --> 00:20:49.370
be useful to look at.

00:20:49.370 --> 00:20:52.550
There's a more specific session
that goes a little bit deeper

00:20:52.550 --> 00:20:56.060
into Filestore Enterprise, which
is something Dave mentioned.

00:20:56.060 --> 00:20:59.000
There's a wider HA and
availability section

00:20:59.000 --> 00:21:01.010
that talks about a number
of the capabilities,

00:21:01.010 --> 00:21:02.840
as well as some
customer sessions

00:21:02.840 --> 00:21:03.920
sprinkled in throughout.

00:21:03.920 --> 00:21:06.962
So I really hope you
get value out of these.

00:21:06.962 --> 00:21:08.420
Included a number
of links to where

00:21:08.420 --> 00:21:11.420
to find best practices
and additional technical

00:21:11.420 --> 00:21:13.790
documentation, as well
as some of the training

00:21:13.790 --> 00:21:16.670
and certification links on how
to get more training and get

00:21:16.670 --> 00:21:17.202
certified.

00:21:17.202 --> 00:21:19.160
And one of the things
that I was excited to see

00:21:19.160 --> 00:21:22.940
is that last year the Google
Cloud Professional Cloud

00:21:22.940 --> 00:21:26.420
Architect was the highest
paying certification.

00:21:26.420 --> 00:21:28.760
So great kind of career
option for people

00:21:28.760 --> 00:21:30.590
to invest in themselves.

00:21:30.590 --> 00:21:31.890
And we're excited.

00:21:31.890 --> 00:21:33.740
Hopefully this session
was a good overview

00:21:33.740 --> 00:21:37.280
of all the things that is what's
new with Google Cloud Storage.

00:21:37.280 --> 00:21:38.510
Enjoy the rest of the event.

00:21:38.510 --> 00:21:39.810
And we'll see you again soon.

00:21:39.810 --> 00:21:41.650
Thank you.

